  <header class="masthead">
    <p class="eyebrow">AskMI · miTch · Repository triage</p>
    <h1>The Sixty-Day Freeze</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">Fifteen pull requests are open and none of them is the problem. <code>master</code> has not moved since 17 July. What looks like fifteen stalled branches is one stalled merge queue, two fixable infrastructure faults, and three branches that were never connected to this repository's history at all.</p>
    <p class="byline">15 September 2026 · evidence gathered against <code>origin/master</code> @ <code>bb34003</code></p>
  </header>

  <dl class="strip">
    <div class="stat"><dt>Since last merge</dt><dd>60 <span>days</span></dd></div>
    <div class="stat"><dt>Open PRs</dt><dd>15</dd></div>
    <div class="stat"><dt>Open &gt; 3 days</dt><dd>9</dd></div>
    <div class="stat"><dt>Disconnected branches</dt><dd>3</dd></div>
  </dl>

  <h2>Abstract</h2>
  <p>The last commit to land on <code>master</code> was <code>bb34003</code> (PR #137) on 17 July 2026. Every pull request opened since then has queued behind it. The backlog reads as chronic — ages from 95 days down to hours — but almost none of it is caused by the work in the branches.</p>
  <p>Three causes account for nearly all of it. A Gemini-powered review workflow has been failing on every pull request since its API quota went to zero, painting the whole board red. Three branches — from two different coding agents — were cut from a foreign git history and share no ancestor with <code>master</code>, so they cannot be rebased at all. And the genuine merge conflicts, when measured rather than assumed, are far smaller than the PR ages suggest: one of them is a single documentation file.</p>
  <p>Six of the seven questions raised by the triage are now settled with direct evidence. The seventh needs one look at the repository settings, and is named explicitly in <a href="#open">Remaining unknown</a>.</p>

  <h2>Findings</h2>

  <div class="finding">
    <div class="fid">F1</div>
    <div>
      <h3>The red CI is a billing fault, not a code fault <span class="verdict v-warn">infrastructure</span></h3>
      <p>The <code>review / review</code> job fails on #138, #145, #146, #147, #148, #150 and #151, and was cancelled on #144. It is not a test. It is <code>.github/workflows/gemini-review.yml</code>, which invokes <code>run-gemini-cli</code> against <code>secrets.GEMINI_API_KEY</code>. The job log ends:</p>
      <div class="cmd">Quota exceeded for metric:
generate_content_free_tier_requests, limit: 0,
model: gemini-3.1-pro  →  status: 429</div>
      <p class="meta">Evidence: job 104484275592 (PR #151). <code>limit: 0</code> means the key has no free-tier allowance at all, so every invocation fails immediately — this will not recover on its own.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <div class="fid">F2</div>
    <div>
      <h3>Three branches have no common ancestor with <code>master</code> <span class="verdict v-stop">structural</span></h3>
      <p>PRs #139 (Codex), #147 and #148 (Jules) all descend from root commit <code>2ad696e</code>. <code>master</code> descends from <code>fb879cc</code>. There is no merge base between them: <code>git merge-tree</code> refuses with <em>"unrelated histories"</em> and <code>git diff master...branch</code> reports <em>"no merge base"</em>.</p>
      <p>GitHub disguises this. It renders #139 as a tidy 9-file diff and #147 as a single new file, but the branches carry 190+ files still using pre-rebrand <code>@mitch/</code> identifiers. These PRs cannot be rebased or updated; their content has to be re-applied onto a branch cut from <code>master</code>.</p>
      <p class="meta">#149, #150 and #151 — also from Jules — are cut correctly from <code>bb34003</code> and carry zero stale identifiers. The defect is per-task, not per-tool.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <div class="fid">F3</div>
    <div>
      <h3>#147's failure is one identifier <span class="verdict v-clear">one-line fix</span></h3>
      <p>The failing <code>Build &amp; Test</code> job is <code>pnpm guard:rebrand</code>, which rejects four stale patterns (<code>@mitch/</code>, <code>did:mitch</code>, <code>mitch.demo</code>, <code>MitchPolicyEvaluator</code>) across <code>src</code>, <code>.github</code> and the root manifests. CI tests the <em>merge</em> result, so <code>master</code>'s clean files win and exactly one violation surfaces:</p>
      <div class="cmd">src/packages/oid4vp/src/__tests__/demo-flow.unit.test.ts:10
  verifierClientId: 'did:mitch:verifier',</div>
      <p class="meta">Verified: <code>master</code> itself has 0 guard violations; PR #147's head has 418. Changing that one identifier to <code>did:askmi:verifier</code> clears the gate — but see F2, the branch still cannot be merged.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <div class="fid">F4</div>
    <div>
